    "result_text": "The full suite passes (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1 test \u2014 the acceptance suite is the only test in the project).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the daily owner-creation limit: **`POST /api/owners` now returns 429 when 100 or more owners already have today's `registrationDate`.**\n\nFollowing the project's OfficeFloor function-injection conventions, I mirrored the existing per-city capacity rule (`EnsureCityHasCapacity` \u2192 `CityAtCapacityException`) rather than inventing a new pattern:\n\n- **`EnsureDailyOwnerLimit`** (function/owner) \u2014 a single-responsibility step that counts existing owners whose `registrationDate` equals `LocalDate.now()` and throws when the count reaches the `DAILY_LIMIT` of 100. It takes only the `OwnerRepository`, so it can run before the new owner is built.\n- **`DailyOwnerLimitException`** (escalation) \u2014 a checked exception, so it surfaces in the `throws` clause.\n- **`DailyOwnerLimitExceptionHandler`** \u2014 responds `429 TOO_MANY_REQUESTS` with a `ProblemDetail`, reusing the shared `ProblemDetails.build(...)` helper like the other handlers.\n- **`...DailyOwnerLimitException.yml`** \u2014 escalation wiring.\n- **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 inserted the `ensureDailyLimit` step (under `transaction` governance) between the city-capacity check and `build`, consistent with the other precondition steps.\n\nI reused `ProblemDetails` and the established step/exception/handler idiom rather than duplicating logic, keeping each unit small and focused.",
